+# Combo variant of b9e38dc0 (index 37)
+# combo_spec: D3/0+D5/0+D12/2
+# Name: 周期涂色+碰撞终止+配对相对
+
+import random
+
+import numpy as np
+from scipy.ndimage import distance_transform_cdt
+
+from arc_utils import components_mask, get_bg, neighbors4, neighbors8
+
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# Design analysis for the combo variant
+#
+# a) Concrete behavior and trace:
+#    We keep the original "funnel enclosure + seed + obstacle shadow" backbone,
+#    but the scene now contains a visible pair of wall sprites that face each
+#    other. Only one member of the pair contains the seed blob; that component
+#    is the active enclosure. Example with an active sprite opening upward:
+#      - Original task: seed flood-fills the interior, then the wedge extends
+#        upward until the canvas border; every painted cell uses one fill color.
+#      - D5 effect: the two boundary rays no longer run to the border. They are
+#        traced step by step and stop as soon as the next step would land on the
+#        opposite-facing partner sprite above.
+#      - D3 effect: after reachability and obstacle shadows are resolved, the
+#        surviving radiated cells outside the opening are recolored in AABB
+#        depth bands: fill, fill, wall, wall, fill, fill, ...
+#      - D12 effect: the blocker is not an arbitrary patch. It is a second
+#        funnel sprite of the same wall color, oriented with the opposite
+#        opening direction so humans can read it as a paired counterpart.
+#    The visible difference from the original is therefore twofold: the wedge
+#    has a finite object-to-object length, and within that finite corridor the
+#    color alternates periodically instead of staying uniform.
+#
+# b) Complexity calibration:
+#    The combo uses only one extra structural degree of freedom beyond the
+#    original task: a second wall component. Its role is heavily constrained:
+#    same wall color, opposite opening direction, placed in front of the active
+#    opening, and required to stop both boundary rays. The periodic coloring is
+#    fixed globally to one pattern (AABB with fill/wall colors), so examples are
+#    varied by corridor length and obstacle placement, not by many unrelated
+#    pattern families.
+#
+# c) Hidden-assumption audit:
+#    - The original `wall = most frequent non-bg color` still works because both
+#      paired sprites share one wall color and dominate the pixel count.
+#    - `wall_comp = closest component to the fill seed` becomes essential: the
+#      seed-bearing enclosure must be the active component, while the opposite
+#      partner acts only as a collision stop.
+#    - The original ray code assumed "stop only at border". That assumption is
+#      invalid now, so rays are explicitly traced and can terminate on another
+#      wall component before leaving the grid.
+#    - The original `out[filled_mask] = fill` assumed a single paint color.
+#      That fails under periodic recoloring, so the solver first computes the
+#      boolean painted region, then applies obstacle shadows, then assigns band
+#      colors by radiate depth.
+#    - BFS still assumes only bg and seed cells are passable. The generator
+#      preserves that by keeping the partner sprite wall-colored rather than
+#      introducing a distinct "stop" color.
+#    - New implicit assumption: the opposite-facing partner must actually cap
+#      the wedge rather than sit off to the side. The generator therefore
+#      searches placements and rejects any case where either boundary ray misses
+#      the partner or the output leaks to the border as if no collision existed.
+#
+# d) Placement legality:
+#    Both sprites are built from the original local wall generator, then one is
+#    rendered with radiate direction `rad` and the partner with `-rad`. The
+#    search enforces in-bounds placement, positive gap along the radiate axis,
+#    8-connectivity separation between the two wall components, and successful
+#    collision hits for both active boundary rays. Seeds are placed only inside
+#    the active enclosure; obstacles are placed on bg cells after nominal fill
+#    reachability is known.
+#
+# e) Visual observability:
+#    A solver seeing 2-4 examples can infer the fused rule from geometry alone:
+#    the seed always sits in one funnel, a second same-colored funnel faces it,
+#    the painted wedge always stops before reaching the border, and the finite
+#    corridor shows repeated fill/wall bands. No ranking by color id or other
+#    hidden numeric property is used.
+#
+# f) Visual neatness:
+#    The generator keeps exactly one paired partner and at most a few singleton
+#    obstacles, so the corridor remains readable. It also rejects placements
+#    where the collision is visually inert, where the radiated region is too
+#    short to reveal the pattern, or where the fill can still spill to a border.
+#
+# g) Color-inference quality:
+#    The periodic colors come from existing semantic colors already exposed by
+#    the puzzle: seed color and wall color. The enclosed interior remains solid
+#    seed color, while only the outward corridor alternates, giving a strong
+#    structural cue for when and where the color rule changes.
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+
+PATTERN_BLOCK = 2  # AABB using fill, fill, wall, wall, ...
